  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "For the function $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y,$ evaluate $\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}$ at the point $(2,\\pi ).$",
  "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y)-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot \\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y)+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y) & =x(\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+y\\cdot \\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y))+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y \\\\  & =x(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+y\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y))+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y-y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} & =2\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1-\\pi \\cdot 0)+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
